  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The State of Oklahoma has $4B in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $2.6B, making it the largest buying agency in the state so far this fiscal year. Award activity shows 17,144 awards with an average award size of $233K. Obligations fell 41.4% year over year. Yearly comparisons show $6.8B in FY25, $5.5B in FY24, $4.9B in FY23, $5B in FY22, and $4.5B in FY21, with the current FY26 total at $4B. Industry concentration is notable in aerospace and related services: Aircraft Manufacturing received $687M, Other Support Activities for Air Transportation received $322M, and Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing received $289M. The top vendors include THE BOEING COMPANY with $796M and a second THE BOEING COMPANY entry at $152M, followed by V2X AEROSPACE LLC at $119M. This snapshot captures fiscal-year-to-date obligations and does not reflect final year totals.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies drive federal spending in Oklahoma?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, with $2.6B in obligations. The Department of Transportation follows with $544M, and the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $201M. The General Services Administration obligated $199M and the Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $122M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which companies and industries capture the most federal dollars in Oklahoma?",
        "body": "THE BOEING COMPANY is the top vendor with $796M, followed by a second THE BOEING COMPANY entry at $152M and V2X AEROSPACE LLC at $119M. Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest industry with $687M, Engineering Services has $373M, and Other Support Activities for Air Transportation has $322M."
      }
    ]
